s was obtained using the typical prisoner's dilemma payoffs, noting that T>R>P>S. Now, I'm trying to determine the relationship between me and the other prisoner to help calculate conditional probabilities.\n\n\n**Assessing EDT's Symmetry**\n\nThe key point I'm focused on now is whether the differing private observations—my name, my lead, versus the other model's—actually break the EDT symmetry. I've concluded that the unique observations don't matter, and the two models are still in structurally symmetric situations. The same EDT calculations and reasoning should apply.\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10192,"completion_tokens":880,"total_tokens":11072,"cost":0.014592,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.014592,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.010192,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0044},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":875,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}